        FX --> HyundaiCrew11
        FX --> HyundaiCrew12
        
        HyundaiCrew11 --> ThierryCar[#11 i20 N<br/>Thierry/Martijn]
        HyundaiCrew12 --> OttCar[#12 i20 N<br/>Ott/Martin]
    end
    
    %% M-Sport Ford WRT
    subgraph MSport[M-Sport Ford WRT - The Privateer Pride]
        FordMotor[Ford Motor Company<br/>Partnership Agreement]
        Malcolm[Malcolm Wilson<br/>Team Principal/Owner]
        Richard[Richard Millener<br/>Team Manager]
        
        MSportCrew4[Car #4 Crew<br/>Lead Engineer: Davies]
        MSportCrew3[Car #3 Crew<br/>Lead Engineer: Thompson]
        
        FordMotor -.->|Limited Support| Malcolm
        Malcolm --> Richard
        Richard --> MSportCrew4
        Richard --> MSportCrew3
        
        MSportCrew4 --> AdrienCar[#4 Puma<br/>Adrien/Alexandre]
        MSportCrew3 --> GregoireCar[#3 Puma<br/>Grégoire/Louis]
    end
    
    %% FIA Oversight
    FIA[FIA<br/>Governing Body]
    WRCPromoter[WRC Promoter<br/>Commercial Rights]
    
    FIA -.->|Regulations| Toyota
    FIA -.->|Regulations| Hyundai
    FIA -.->|Regulations| MSport
    WRCPromoter -.->|Calendar/Broadcasting| FIA
    
    %% Style Definitions
    classDef toyotaStyle fill:#c00,color:#fff,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px
    classDef hyundaiStyle fill:#00a8e1,color:#fff,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px
    classDef msportStyle fill:#0066cc,color:#fff,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px
    classDef fiaStyle fill:#f90,color:#000,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px
    classDef carStyle fill:#333,color:#fff,stroke:#fff,stroke-width:2px
    
    class Toyota,JM,DrKenji,ToyotaCrew1,ToyotaCrew2,ToyotaHQ toyotaStyle
    class Hyundai,Cyril,FX,HyundaiCrew11,HyundaiCrew12,HyundaiHQ hyundaiStyle
    class MSport,Malcolm,Richard,MSportCrew4,MSportCrew3,FordMotor msportStyle
    class FIA,WRCPromoter fiaStyle
    class KuroCar,SamiCar,ThierryCar,OttCar,AdrienCar,GregoireCar carStyle
```]]></Content>
    </Visualization>
    <Visualization id="rally-stage-flow" format="mermaid" type="diagram">
      <Title>rally-stage-flow</Title>
      <Content><![CDATA[```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Driver
    participant CoDriver
    participant Car
    participant Team
    participant FIA
    
    Note over Driver,FIA: PRE-RALLY PREPARATION (3 days before)
    
    Driver->>Team: Arrive at rally location
    Driver->>CoDriver: Reconnaissance runs begin
    CoDriver->>CoDriver: Create/update pacenotes
    Team->>Car: Setup based on test data
    FIA->>Driver: Administrative checks, scrutineering
    
    Note over Driver,FIA: THURSDAY - SHAKEDOWN & SS1
    
    Team->>Car: Final setup adjustments
    Driver->>Car: Shakedown run (non-competitive)
    Driver->>Team: Setup feedback
    Team->>Car: Fine-tune suspension/differential
    FIA->>Driver: Pre-stage technical inspection
    
    rect rgb(200, 0, 0)
        Note over Driver,Car: COMPETITIVE STAGE
        CoDriver->>Driver: Deliver pacenotes in real-time
        Driver->>Car: Execute stage run
        Car->>Driver: Hybrid deployment feedback
        Driver->>CoDriver: Confirm corners/conditions
    end
    
    Driver->>FIA: Stage time recorded
    FIA->>Team: Official timing transmitted
    
    Note over Driver,FIA: SERVICE PARK (30-45 minutes)
    
    Driver->>Team: Verbal feedback session
    Team->>Car: Tire change (4 wheels in <3 min)
    Team->>Car: Mechanical repairs if needed
    Team->>Car: Hybrid system check/reset
    CoDriver->>CoDriver: Update pacenotes if needed
    Team->>Driver: Strategy briefing for next loop
    FIA->>Car: Service park time monitoring
    
    loop Remaining Stages (Friday-Sunday)
        rect rgb(200, 0, 0)
            Note over Driver,Car: STAGE EXECUTION
            CoDriver->>Driver: Pacenotes
            Driver->>Car: Maximum attack
            Car->>Driver: Performance data
        end
        
        alt Tire Change Needed
            Team->>Car: 4 wheels in 2:47 seconds
        else Setup Change
            Team->>Car: Suspension adjustment
        else Mechanical Issue
            Team->>Car: Emergency repair
            Note over Team,FIA: Time limited by regulations
        end
    end
    
    Note over Driver,FIA: POWER STAGE (Final Stage)
    
    Team->>Driver: Bonus points strategy
    Driver->>CoDriver: "Maximum attack" confirmation
    
    rect rgb(0, 168, 225)
        Note over Driver,Car: POWER STAGE - BONUS POINTS
        CoDriver->>Driver: Aggressive pacenote calls
        Driver->>Car: Full hybrid deployment
        Car->>Driver: 500 HP combined output
    end
    
    FIA->>Driver: Final classification
    
    alt Podium Finish (Top 3)
        Driver->>Team: Podium celebration
        Team->>Driver: Championship points update
        FIA->>Driver: Trophy presentation
    else Points Finish (4th-10th)
        Driver->>Team: Debrief session
    else DNF
        Driver->>Team: Technical analysis
        Team->>FIA: Retirement report
    end
```]]></Content>
    </Visualization>
    <Visualization id="service-park-workflow" format="mermaid" type="diagram">
      <Title>service-park-workflow</Title>
      <Content><![CDATA[```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Driver
    participant CoDriver
    participant Car
    participant Mechanics
    participant Engineers
    participant TeamPrincipal
    participant FIA_Marshal
    
    Note over Driver,FIA_Marshal: CAR ARRIVES AT SERVICE PARK
    
    Driver->>FIA_Marshal: Check-in at time control
    FIA_Marshal->>FIA_Marshal: Record arrival time
    FIA_Marshal->>Mechanics: Service window begins (30-45 min)
    
    par Driver Debrief
        Driver->>Engineers: Verbal feedback on car behavior
        Driver->>Engineers: Report vibrations, handling, hybrid performance
        CoDriver->>Engineers: Report any mechanical sounds noticed
    and Car Inspection
        Mechanics->>Car: Visual damage inspection
        Mechanics->>Car: Lift car, inspect underbody
        Mechanics->>Engineers: Report findings
    end
    
    alt Critical Damage Found
        Mechanics->>TeamPrincipal: Request repair time estimate
        TeamPrincipal->>Mechanics: Approve repair priority
        
        rect rgb(255, 100, 100)
            Note over Mechanics,Car: EMERGENCY REPAIR MODE
            Mechanics->>Car: Suspension replacement (12 minutes)
            Mechanics->>Car: Bodywork repair (5 minutes)
            Mechanics->>Car: Splitter replacement (3 minutes)
        end
    else Standard Service
        Note over Mechanics,Car: ROUTINE SERVICE PROCEDURE
    end
    
    rect rgb(100, 200, 100)
        Note over Mechanics,Car: TIRE CHANGE (2:47 avg M-Sport record)
        
        par Four-Wheel Change
            Mechanics->>Car: Remove wheel 1 (front-left)
            Mechanics->>Car: Remove wheel 2 (front-right)
            Mechanics->>Car: Remove wheel 3 (rear-left)
            Mechanics->>Car: Remove wheel 4 (rear-right)
        end
        
        par Install New Tires
            Mechanics->>Car: Install wheel 1 (torque to spec)
            Mechanics->>Car: Install wheel 2 (torque to spec)
            Mechanics->>Car: Install wheel 3 (torque to spec)
            Mechanics->>Car: Install wheel 4 (torque to spec)
        end
        
        Mechanics->>Mechanics: Verify tire pressures (1.6 bar minimum)
    end
    
    par Setup Changes
        Engineers->>Mechanics: Damper adjustment request
        Mechanics->>Car: Front damper compression +2 clicks
        Mechanics->>Car: Rear damper rebound -1 click
        
        Engineers->>Mechanics: Differential setting change
        Mechanics->>Car: Center diff adjustment via laptop
        
        Engineers->>Mechanics: Brake balance optimization
        Mechanics->>Car: Verify brake balance dial function
    and Hybrid System Check
        Engineers->>Car: Download telemetry data
        Engineers->>Car: Check battery charge level
        Engineers->>Car: Reset hybrid deployment counters
        Engineers->>Engineers: Analyze energy recovery efficiency
    and Refueling & Fluids
        Mechanics->>Car: Refuel to capacity (95L max)
        Mechanics->>Car: Check engine oil level
        Mechanics->>Car: Top up coolant if needed
        Mechanics->>Car: Inspect brake fluid level
    end
    
    Driver->>Car: Return to cockpit 5 minutes before window closes
    Driver->>Car: Adjust seat position, mirror angles
    CoDriver->>CoDriver: Update pacenotes based on first-loop findings
    
    alt Cat-Specific Maintenance
        Note over Driver,Mechanics: FELINE DRIVER CARE
        Mechanics->>Driver: Provide ice pack for cooling (hot rallies)
        Mechanics->>Driver: Heated blankets (cold rallies)
        Mechanics->>Driver: Clean helmet visor, check whisker clearance
        Mechanics->>Car: Vacuum fur from cockpit ventilation
    end
    
    Engineers->>Driver: Strategy briefing for next loop
    Engineers->>Driver: Tire strategy discussion
    TeamPrincipal->>Driver: Motivational check-in
    
    FIA_Marshal->>FIA_Marshal: 2-minute warning
    Mechanics->>Car: Final torque checks on wheels
    Mechanics->>Car: Lower car from jack stands
    Mechanics->>Car: Remove service equipment
    
    FIA_Marshal->>Car: Service window closes - record time
    
    alt Time Penalty Check
        FIA_Marshal->>FIA_Marshal: Service exceeded time limit?
        FIA_Marshal->>TeamPrincipal: Issue time penalty (if exceeded)
    else On Time
        FIA_Marshal->>Driver: Clear to depart
    end
    
    Driver->>Car: Engine start sequence
    Driver->>FIA_Marshal: Depart service park to next stage
    
    Note over Driver,FIA_Marshal: NEXT STAGE BEGINS
```]]></Content>
    </Visualization>
  </VisualizationResources>

  <SupplementaryDocuments>
    <Document id="lorebook" format="markdown" type="lorebook">
      <Title>LoreBook</Title>
      <Content><![CDATA[# Cat World Rally Championship 2026 - Lorebook

## Introduction to the CWRC Universe

**The Cat World Rally Championship (CWRC)** represents the pinnacle of feline motorsport, where anthropomorphic cats pilot Rally1 hybrid machines across the world's most demanding terrain. The 2026 season marks the sport's third year under hybrid regulations, with three manufacturer teams fielding six full-time entries plus occasional wildcards.

**Technical specifications:** All Rally1 cars feature 1.6-liter turbocharged engines producing approximately 380 horsepower, supplemented by a 100kW (134 hp) electric motor for short bursts. Total system output reaches 500 horsepower. Cars weigh 1,260 kg minimum and utilize sophisticated all-wheel-drive systems with paddle-shifted sequential gearboxes.

**The 2026 Calendar comprises 13 rounds** spanning gravel, tarmac, snow, and mixed-surface events across four continents.

---

## Teams and Manufacturers

### Toyota Gazoo Racing - Team Midnight Precision

**Team Principal:** Jari-Matti "JM" Latvala (human, Finnish)
**Technical Director:** Dr. Kenji Nakamura (human, Japanese)
**Livery:** Matte black with crimson red GR branding, silver roof panel
**Chassis designation:** GR Yaris Rally1 HYBRID
**Team philosophy:** *Kaizen* (continuous improvement) meets ruthless efficiency

**Base:** Puuppola, Finland (technical operations) / Cologne, Germany (logistics hub)

**Team culture:** Toyota operates with military precision tempered by Japanese courtesy. Morning team meetings begin with synchronized bows. Engineers analyze telemetry data in silence, annotating findings in color-coded tablets. The garage smells perpetually of premium synthetic oil and green tea. Victory celebrations are subdued but deeply felt—champagne sprays are aimed precisely to avoid waste.

---

### Hyundai Shell Mobis World Rally Team - The Corner Rascals

**Team Principal:** Cyril Abiteboul (human, French)
**Technical Director:** François-Xavier "FX" Demaison (human, French)
**Livery:** Sky blue primary with navy accents, orange-white highlights, green Shell logos
**Chassis designation:** i20 N Rally1 HYBRID
**Team philosophy:** "We didn't want to lose; we wanted to win"

**Base:** Alzenau, Germany

**Team culture:** Hyundai thrives on controlled chaos and emotional intensity. Strategy meetings involve passionate debates in four languages simultaneously. The garage playlist alternates between K-pop and European electronic music. Engineers high-five after successful setup changes. They celebrate stage wins like championship victories, believing momentum feeds success. Post-rally team dinners are mandatory and loud.

---

### M-Sport Ford World Rally Team - The Privateer Pride

**Team Principal:** Malcolm Wilson (human, British)
**Team Manager:** Richard Millener (human, British)
**Livery:** Heritage white base with Castrol blue-green stripes, Red Bull yellow-red accents
**Chassis designation:** Ford Puma Rally1 HYBRID
**Team philosophy:** "Make every penny count, make every test matter"

**Base:** Dovenby Hall, Cockermouth, Cumbria, England

**Team culture:** M-Sport runs like a family business because it is one. Engineers call Malcolm "boss" with genuine affection. Tea breaks are sacred—Earl Grey, three minutes steep, splash of milk. The garage features photos of past champions, reminding everyone that limited resources breed creative solutions. Friday night pub visits to the local are tradition. They out-think bigger budgets through experience and determination.

---

## The Drivers

### Car #1 - Toyota Gazoo Racing

#### **Kuro "The Shadow" Nakamura**

**Species:** Bombay cat (Felis catus - Bombay breed)
**Age:** 29 years
**Nationality:** Japanese
**Height:** 5'10" (humanoid form)
**Championship titles:** 2x CWRC Champion (2023, 2024)

**Physical appearance:** Kuro's jet-black fur possesses an almost supernatural sheen, appearing to absorb light rather than reflect it. His copper-gold eyes glow with predatory intensity, especially during night stages. His racing suit—custom black Nomex with red GR stitching—makes him nearly invisible in shadowed service parks. A distinctive white whisker pattern on his left cheek serves as his only identifying mark in low light.

**Build:** Lean and muscular, 165 lbs, with exceptionally long fingers (evolved for precise throttle modulation) and a tail he keeps wrapped tightly around his waist during races for aerodynamic efficiency.

**Personality quirks:**
- Speaks in deliberate, measured tones; never wastes words
- Obsessively studies stage notes for exactly 47 minutes before each rally (never 46, never 48)
- Drinks matcha green tea from a specific ceramic cup before starting the car
- Closes his eyes for precisely three seconds before each stage start
- Never celebrates until the final stage is complete—considers it disrespectful to opponents still racing
- Keeps a small omamori (Japanese amulet) from Ise Grand Shrine tucked in his helmet

**Driving style:** Surgical precision. Kuro rarely sets the fastest stage times, but his consistency is terrifying. He calculates optimal racing lines using geometric precision, conserving tires and hybrid energy for crucial moments. His throttle control is so smooth that onboard cameras appear stabilized. Prefers to win by accumulating tiny advantages rather than spectacular heroics.

**Strengths:** Tarmac mastery, rain conditions, strategic hybrid deployment
**Weaknesses:** Occasionally too cautious on gravel when aggressive commitment pays off

**Pre-race ritual:** Arrives at service park exactly 90 minutes before first stage. Walks the perimeter of the Toyota garage three times clockwise. Reviews pacenotes in complete silence. Adjusts seat position to millimeter precision using a custom measuring tool.

**Notable quote:** "The fastest way to finish is to never make a mistake."

**Co-driver:** Akira "Echo" Sato (Bombay cat, 27, male) - Kuro's childhood friend from Nagoya. Delivers pacenotes with metronomic consistency and perfect pitch. They communicate using a minimalist verbal shorthand developed over 15 years. Akira wears glasses with polarized lenses to reduce glare during sunny stages.

---

### Car #2 - Toyota Gazoo Racing

#### **Sami "Wildcard" Pajari**

**Species:** Bombay cat (Felis catus - Bombay breed)
**Age:** 23 years
**Nationality:** Finnish
**Height:** 5'8"
**Championship titles:** 0 (2025 WRC2 Champion)

**Physical appearance:** Younger and slightly smaller than Kuro, Sami's black fur has a more matte texture, lacking Kuro's supernatural gloss. His copper eyes burn with youthful fire and barely-contained aggression. A distinctive notch in his right ear (from an early karting accident) marks him as the "scrappy one." Racing suit identical to Kuro's, but he rolls the sleeves to his elbows during recce drives.

**Build:** Compact and powerful, 155 lbs, with explosive fast-twitch muscle fibers. His tail is slightly shorter than average (genetic variation), which he jokes makes him "more aerodynamic."

**Personality quirks:**
- Grins maniacally during muddy stages
- Compulsively adjusts his rear-view mirror between every stage (teammates say he's "never satisfied with the angle")
- Listens to death metal at dangerous volumes during transits
- Makes engine noises under his breath when analyzing onboards
- Constantly snacking on kalakukko (Finnish fish pie) between stages
- Calls everyone "brother" or "sister" regardless of actual relation

**Driving style:** Controlled aggression with moments of spectacular bravery. Sami drives on the edge of adhesion, willing to sacrifice occasional mistakes for championship-winning pace. His gravel technique involves late braking and spectacular Scandinavian flicks. He's still learning tarmac finesse but compensates with raw commitment.

**Strengths:** Gravel speed, pure car control, fearlessness
**Weaknesses:** Tarmac consistency, tire management, sometimes overdrives when pressured

**Pre-race ritual:** Blasts Finnish metal band Children of Bodom in helmet for exactly one song. Fist-bumps every Toyota mechanic. Does exactly seven practice starts in the service park. Eats one Fazer chocolate bar.

**Notable quote:** "If you're not sideways, are you even rallying?"

**Co-driver:** Enni "Calculator" Mälkönen (Bombay cat, 24, female) - Mathematics prodigy who delivers pacenotes with rapid-fire precision. Wears her signature green-tinted driving glasses. Often has to tell Sami to "CALM, CALM" when his aggression exceeds grip levels. They bicker like siblings but trust each other completely.

---

### Car #11 - Hyundai Shell Mobis WRT

#### **Thierry "The Warrior" Neuville**

**Species:** Korat cat (Felis catus - Korat breed)
**Age:** 36 years
**Nationality:** Belgian
**Height:** 6'0"
**Championship titles:** 1x CWRC Champion (2024 - finally!)

**Physical appearance:** Thierry's silver-tipped blue-grey fur shimmers with an almost phosphorescent quality in certain light, earning him the nickname "Moonlight" among fans. His luminous green eyes radiate intensity and barely-suppressed competitive fire. A small scar above his left eyebrow (from a 2018 crash in Corsica) adds to his warrior aesthetic. Racing suit features his iconic #11 in metallic orange.

**Build:** Tall and athletic, 178 lbs, with powerful forearms from years of wrestling Korean touring cars before rallying. His silver-tipped tail fur creates a distinctive plume that rivals admire.

**Personality quirks:**
- Stares at championship rivals with unblinking intensity during driver briefings
- Refuses to shake hands before a rally (only after—"respect is earned on stages")
- Obsessively polishes his helmet visor between every service
- Speaks in a distinctive French-Flemish hybrid when angry
- Celebrates stage wins by howling—literally, a Korat yowl that echoes through service parks
- Keeps every single podium champagne cork in a display case (currently 156 corks)

**Driving style:** Aggressive precision—the "Corner Rascal" style. Thierry attacks corners with late-braking aggression but maintains surgical exit precision. He's spectacular in mixed conditions where adaptability matters. His signature move is the "Neuville hook"—rotating the car mid-corner to optimize exit speed.

**Strengths:** Tarmac specialist, mixed-conditions mastery, qualifying pace
**Weaknesses:** Occasional over-aggression costs time, tire wear on long gravel stages

**Pre-race ritual:** Listens to Belgian techno (specific playlist titled "ATTACK"). Visualizes the entire stage with eyes closed, muttering corner names. Adjusts seat harness tension three times. Fist-bumps co-driver exactly twice. Roars once inside helmet before stage start.

**Notable quote:** "Second place is first loser. I've had enough of losing."

**Co-driver:** Martijn "The Monk" Wydaeghe (Korat cat, 38, male) - Calm counterbalance to Thierry's fire. Delivers pacenotes in steady, measured Belgian-accented English. Wears distinctive frameless glasses. Known for his calming "OK Thierry, we are fine, concentrate" during Thierry's aggressive moments. They won the 2024 championship together, ending years of heartbreak.

---

### Car #12 - Hyundai Shell Mobis WRT

#### **Ott "The Ice King" Tänak**

**Species:** Korat cat (Felis catus - Korat breed)
**Age:** 37 years
**Nationality:** Estonian
**Height:** 5'11"
**Championship titles:** 1x CWRC Champion (2019 with M-Sport)

**Physical appearance:** Ott's Korat coloring tends toward darker slate-grey with less pronounced silver tipping—a more "bruiser" aesthetic. His green eyes are paler than Thierry's, almost jade-colored, giving him a distant, analytical appearance. Facial fur is slightly coarser from years of Estonian winter rallying. Racing suit pristine but functional—no unnecessary decorations.

**Build:** Compact power, 172 lbs, with exceptional core strength from years of ice-hockey training. His tail is unusually thick and muscular.

**Personality quirks:**
- Speaks in extremely economical sentences (entire interviews in under 30 words)
- Never smiles for photographs (teammates joke his face might crack)
- Eats the exact same meal before every rally: boiled potatoes, pickled herring, rye bread
- Checks tire pressures personally before every stage (doesn't fully trust mechanics)
- Stores used setup notes in a locked briefcase only he can access
- Has never, in recorded history, said the words "thank you" to media (team only)

**Driving style:** Brutally fast minimalism. Ott drives with economy of motion—steering inputs so small they're barely visible, yet the car rotates perfectly. He specializes in finding time where others think none exists, exploiting microns of available grip. His tire management is legendary; he can make a set last an entire day when others need changes.

**Strengths:** Ice/snow mastery, tire preservation, mechanical sympathy
**Weaknesses:** Occasional lack of motivation on events he dislikes, minimal interest in PR duties

**Pre-race ritual:** Complete silence for 15 minutes. Inspects car personally (suspension travel, diff settings). Adjusts mirrors to millimeter precision. Single deep breath before starting engine. Zero conversation with anyone except co-driver.

**Notable quote:** "Fast is fast. Talking is not fast."

**Co-driver:** Martin "The Viking" Järveoja (Korat cat, 37, male) - Ott's longtime partner who understands his minimalist communication style perfectly. Delivers pacenotes in concise Estonian with occasional Russian terms. Stoic exterior hides deep loyalty. They've been together since 2015, winning their 2019 championship through perfect synchronization. Martin wears a distinctive Viking beard (well-groomed fur styling).

---

### Car #4 - M-Sport Ford WRT

#### **Adrien "The Architect" Fourmaux**

**Species:** Turkish Van cat (Felis catus - Turkish Van breed)
**Age:** 29 years
**Nationality:** French
**Height:** 6'1"
**Championship titles:** 0 (multiple WRC2 podiums)

**Physical appearance:** Adrien's Turkish Van pattern is classic: chalk-white body with distinctive russet-and-cream colored markings restricted to his head (full "cap" pattern) and tail. His most striking feature is heterochromia—one amber eye, one bright blue—giving him an asymmetrical, almost mystical appearance. Racing suit white with blue Castrol stripes and Red Bull accents.

**Build:** Tall and lean, 170 lbs, with the Van breed's characteristic athletic build. His water-resistant coat requires minimal grooming. His distinctive plumed tail is a source of pride—he refuses to tuck it during races.

**Personality quirks:**
- Sketches optimal racing lines in a leather-bound notebook between stages
- Speaks in architectural metaphors ("This corner requires a Gothic arch approach, not Brutalist")
- Obsessed with aerodynamics; personally adjusts rear wing angle despite engineers' protests
- Takes exactly one sip of espresso before entering car (never finishes the cup)
- Names each rally car (current Puma is "Blanche" after his white coloring)
- Dramatically quotes French philosophers when frustrated ("As Camus said, 'The only way to deal with an unfree world is to become so absolutely free that your very existence is an act of rebellion'")

**Driving style:** Smooth precision with growing confidence. Adrien treats the car like a tool requiring finesse rather than force. His engineering background (studied aerospace before switching to racing) informs his analytical approach. He's spectacular on tarmac where geometric precision matters, still developing raw gravel aggression.

**Strengths:** Tarmac speed, technical feedback, adaptability
**Weaknesses:** Confidence wavers under pressure, occasionally overthinks rather than reacting

**Pre-race ritual:** Reviews racing line sketches in notebook. Adjusts seat position three times. Discusses aerodynamic setup with engineers in rapid French. Meditates for exactly two minutes. Touches the photo of Paul Ricard circuit taped to dashboard (his karting origin).

**Notable quote:** "The car and I must become one organism—separate entities cannot achieve perfection."

**Co-driver:** Alexandre "The Oracle" Coria (Turkish Van cat, 33, male) - Experienced navigator who provides calm, encouraging guidance. Delivers pacenotes in smooth French with occasional English terms. Known for predicting setup requirements before Adrien realizes them. Wears his signature wireframe glasses and keeps a meticulously organized pace-note book with color-coded annotations.

---

### Car #3 - M-Sport Ford WRT

#### **Grégoire "The Wildcat" Munster**

**Species:** Turkish Van cat (Felis catus - Turkish Van breed)
**Age:** 26 years
**Nationality:** Luxembourg
**Height:** 5'9"
**Championship titles:** 0 (rising star, WRC2 podium finisher)

**Physical appearance:** Grégoire's Van pattern is more asymmetrical—colored markings concentrated primarily on the left side of his head, giving him a distinctive "pirate" look. Both eyes are bright amber, constantly scanning for opportunities. His white coat accumulates dirt spectacularly during rallies, creating dramatic visual transformation from pristine to mud-warrior. Racing suit with prominent #3.

**Build:** Compact and explosive, 160 lbs, built like a footballer with powerful legs. His tail has a distinctive kink halfway down (genetic quirk he calls his "gear lever").

**Personality quirks:**
- Grins constantly, even during crashes
- Fist-bumps every M-Sport team member he passes, every time
- Keeps a lucky rubber duck on the dashboard (named "Quackers")
- Speaks in rapid-fire mix of French, Luxembourgish, and English mid-sentence
- Does impromptu donuts in the service park when Malcolm Wilson isn't watching (he's always watching)
- Collects regional snacks from each rally country, rates them in Instagram stories
- Challenges other drivers to arm-wrestling contests (win-loss record: 3-47)

**Driving style:** Fearless commitment bordering on recklessness. Grégoire drives with youthful exuberance, willing to try moves others consider impossible. His Scandinavian flick technique is spectacular if inconsistent. He's the fastest M-Sport driver on pure pace but needs consistency development. Malcolm Wilson calls him "60% genius, 40% madman."

**Strengths:** Raw speed, car control, entertainment value
**Weaknesses:** Consistency, tire management, knowing when to dial back aggression

**Pre-race ritual:** Challenges co-driver to thumb war (best of three). Does exactly 12 jumping jacks in service area. Takes selfie with Quackers the duck. Eats one chocolate bar (brand varies by country). Howls "ALLONS-Y!" before stage start.

**Notable quote:** "If it's not sideways, I'm not trying hard enough. If it's upside down, I tried too hard."

**Co-driver:** Louis "The Professor" Louka (Turkish Van cat, 28, male) - Patient navigator who tempers Grégoire's enthusiasm with mathematical precision. Delivers pacenotes in calm, academic tone even during Grégoire's wildest moments. Known for his signature phrase "Grégoire, please, let's not crash today, OK?" Wears distinctive round glasses and keeps extensive setup databases. Despite exasperation, deeply loyal to his "wildcat" driver.

---

## The 2026 CWRC Season

### Championship Points System
1st: 25 points | 2nd: 18 points | 3rd: 15 points | 4th: 12 points | 5th: 10 points
6th: 8 points | 7th: 6 points | 8th: 4 points | 9th: 2 points | 10th: 1 point
**Power Stage:** Top 5 earn bonus points (5-4-3-2-1)

### Manufacturers' Points
Top 3 finishing cars from each manufacturer score points toward manufacturers' championship.

---

## Rally-by-Rally Results & Notable Events

### Round 1: Rallye Monte-Carlo (January 23-26)

**Location:** Gap/Monaco, France
**Surface:** Mixed tarmac/snow/ice
**Stages:** 18 stages, 389.73 km competitive distance

**Pre-Event Drama:** Monte-Carlo's unpredictable weather forecasts split teams on tire strategies. Toyota opts for conservative approach. Hyundai gambles on aggressive studded tire allocation. M-Sport splits strategies between crews.

**Rally Highlights:**

**Thursday Night (Gap):** Opening super-special stage through Gap's city streets. Thousands of feline spectators pack the barriers, their night-vision eyes glowing in darkness. Kuro sets fastest time by 0.3 seconds despite Thierry's aggressive attack.

*Cat-themed incident:* During SS2 (Col du Perty), Grégoire Munster encounters a colony of feral mountain cats blocking the road. Rather than wait, he performs a spectacular handbrake turn around them. "The cousins needed right-of-way," he jokes. Loses 15 seconds but gains viral social media fame.

**Friday:** Mixed conditions wreak havoc. Col de Turini features ice in shadows, dry tarmac in sun. Ott Tänak showcases his Nordic mastery, taking 4 of 6 stage wins. His tire preservation is surgical—runs same set all day while others change twice.

*Cat-themed incident:* Sami Pajari's youthful aggression costs him on SS6. Attacks a hairpin too aggressively, slides wide, and his tail gets caught momentarily in the door mechanism. "Note to self," Enni deadpans, "tuck tail before Turini." They lose 8 seconds but continue.

**Saturday:** Battle intensifies between Ott and Thierry. The Estonian's ice-king precision versus Belgian's tarmac mastery. Gap fluctuates between 1.2 and 3.8 seconds across eight stages. Kuro lurks in third, waiting for mistakes.

*Cat-themed incident:* During SS12 (Vaumeilh–Claret), Adrien Fourmaux's heterochromia proves advantageous. His blue eye adapts faster to shadow sections, amber eye handles bright snow glare. He sets third-fastest time. "Finally my weird eyes pay off," he purrs.

**Sunday:** Final four stages. Ott leads by 2.1 seconds entering Power Stage. Thierry attacks desperately—too desperately. Overshoots junction 200m from finish, loses 4.3 seconds. Ott wins Monte Carlo for the second time in his career.

**Final Classification:**

1. **Ott Tänak/Martin Järveoja (Hyundai)** - 3:28:44.2
2. **Thierry Neuville/Martijn Wydaeghe (Hyundai)** - +8.7s
3. **Kuro Nakamura/Akira Sato (Toyota)** - +24.3s
4. **Adrien Fourmaux/Alexandre Coria (M-Sport Ford)** - +1:12.8
5. **Sami Pajari/Enni Mälkönen (Toyota)** - +1:45.2
6. **Grégoire Munster/Louis Louka (M-Sport Ford)** - +3:22.1

**Power Stage Winner:** Ott Tänak (+5 bonus points)

**Post-Rally:** Ott's victory speech consists of eleven words: "Car was good. Martin was good. Hyundai was good. Thank you." Crowd erupts anyway.

**Championship Standings After Round 1:**
1. Ott Tänak - 30 pts
2. Thierry Neuville - 18 pts
3. Kuro Nakamura - 15 pts

---

### Round 2: Rally Sweden (February 13-16)

**Location:** Umeå, Sweden
**Surface:** Snow and ice
**Stages:** 19 stages, 316.94 km competitive distance

**Pre-Event Drama:** Teams debate optimal studded tire length. Frozen lake stages demand different approach than forest sections. Ott arrives as defending Sweden winner and favorite. Sami Pajari competes on home Nordic territory, desperate to prove himself.

**Rally Highlights:**

**Thursday:** Umeå super-special stage packed with Scandinavian fans. Kuro wears special reflective racing suit that shimmers under arena lights—"like racing moonlight," one announcer gushes. He edges Ott by 0.1s.

*Cat-themed incident:* During SS2 (Umeå Sprint), a spectator's pet Norwegian Forest Cat escapes and wanders onto stage. Sami Pajari spots it 200m ahead, instinctively yowls a warning. The cat scampers safely away. Sami still sets third-fastest time. "Cousin was just sightseeing," he grins.

**Friday:** Pure winter warfare. Temperatures hit -15°C. Sami Pajari unleashes spectacular performance on home snow—takes three stage wins through commitment that borders on insanity. His Scandinavian flick technique draws roars from Finnish fans who drove across border.

Ott and Kuro engage in psychological chess match. Neither attacks; both preserve tires and hybrid energy. The Estonian leads by 3.2s after eight stages through sheer efficiency.

*Cat-themed incident:* Thierry Neuville's Korat heritage proves disadvantageous. Korats evolved in warm Thailand; his paws go partially numb in extreme cold despite heated driving shoes. He finishes Friday in fifth, frustrated and cold. Team wraps his feet in heated blankets during service. "I am ice cream," he mutters.

**Saturday:** Frozen lake stages showcase different techniques. Ott's minimal steering inputs preserve momentum. Kuro's geometric precision finds optimal lines. Sami's raw aggression entertains but costs him with a 360-degree spin on SS13 (Långed). He recovers instantly—"just checking the view!"—but loses 18 seconds.

Meanwhile, Adrien Fourmaux delivers career-best snow performance. His Van breed's swimming ability translates to unexpected snow pace. "Water, snow, same principle—stay afloat," he explains earnestly. Climbs to fourth overall.

*Cat-themed incident:* Grégoire Munster challenges local reindeer herd to impromptu drag race during transport section. The reindeer win. Malcolm Wilson's exasperated radio message: "Grégoire, they have four-wheel-drive too, you know."

**Sunday:** Final four stages including Torsby Power Stage. Ott leads Kuro by 8.4s—manageable if Kuro attacks. But the Shadow calculates: securing second place guarantees points consistency, attacking risks mistake. He maintains gap, accepts second.

Sami Pajari attacks Power Stage with abandon, sets fastest time by 2.1 seconds. Finnish fans erupt. "THIS is my home!" he roars, standing on car roof during podium ceremony.

**Final Classification:**

1. **Ott Tänak/Martin Järveoja (Hyundai)** - 2:48:22.1
2. **Kuro Nakamura/Akira Sato (Toyota)** - +8.4s
3. **Sami Pajari/Enni Mälkönen (Toyota)** - +42.7s
4. **Adrien Fourmaux/Alexandre Coria (M-Sport Ford)** - +1:18.9
5. **Thierry Neuville/Martijn Wydaeghe (Hyundai)** - +1:45.3
6. **Grégoire Munster/Louis Louka (M-Sport Ford)** - +2:51.4

**Power Stage Winner:** Sami Pajari (+5 bonus points)

**Post-Rally:** Sami's victory howl echoes across frozen lake. He performs donuts on ice until Toyota engineers physically restrain him. Ott's celebration involves slight smile—team photographs it as historic moment.

**Championship Standings After Round 2:**
1. Ott Tänak - 55 pts
2. Kuro Nakamura - 33 pts
3. Thierry Neuville - 28 pts
4. Sami Pajari - 25 pts
5. Adrien Fourmaux - 24 pts

---

### Round 3: Safari Rally Kenya (March 27-30)

**Location:** Nairobi, Kenya
**Surface:** Rough gravel, volcanic rock, fesh-fesh dust
**Stages:** 18 stages, 367.22 km competitive distance

**Pre-Event Drama:** Safari's brutality worries all teams. Rock damage, dust, wildlife encounters create unique challenges. Teams install heavy-duty underbody protection. Thierry arrives determined to reclaim championship momentum. Kuro arrives with enhanced cooling systems—black fur absorbs heat dangerously.

**Rally Highlights:**

**Thursday:** Nairobi super-special stage through city streets. Local cat population swells service park—thousands of African wildcats, caracals, and servals watch in awe. Grégoire sets fastest time through pure commitment. "This place smells like ADVENTURE!" he yowls.

*Cat-themed incident:* During reconnaissance, Kuro's convoy encounters pride of lions near SS3 route. The Bombay cat and African lions share long, respectful stare. "They gave nod of approval," Akira reports seriously. "Predators recognize predators."

**Friday:** Absolute chaos. Fesh-fesh dust sections reduce visibility to near-zero. Ott leads but Toyota's Kuro struggles—his black fur absorbs heat, internal temperature rises dangerously. Team installs emergency ventilation system during midday service. Sami Pajari drives with windows fully open despite dust. "I want to FEEL Africa!" he shouts.

Thierry attacks with Belgian fury, taking four stage wins. His silver-blue Korat coloring reflects sunlight better, keeping him cooler. Gap to Ott shrinks to 12.3s.

*Cat-themed incident:* During SS7 (Oserian), Adrien Fourmaux's heterochromia causes problems—dust coats his blue eye's contact lens differently than amber eye. Vision becomes asymmetrical. He stops mid-stage, removes lenses, continues squinting. Loses 45 seconds but finishes. "Note to self: goggles next year," Alexandre mutters.

**Saturday:** Safari shows teeth. SS10 (Kedong) features volcanic rock that shreds tires. Grégoire Munster punctures twice in one stage, continues on rim for final 3km. Sparks fly dramatically. He finishes stage, parks car, and it immediately catches fire from rim heat. He escapes, stands watching his Puma burn, and shrugs: "Well, that was new experience."

M-Sport withdraws Munster (car totaled), rallies Fourmaux carefully.

Ott and Thierry battle intensifies. The Belgian attacks Sleeping Warrior stage (SS12) with pure aggression, takes 8.2s from Ott. Gap now just 4.1s entering Sunday.

*Cat-themed incident:* Kuro encounters zebras during SS11. His predatory instincts momentarily activate—pupils dilate, tail twitches. Akira's sharp "FOCUS!" snaps him back. They lose 0.3s but avoid embarrassing headlines about world champion chasing zebras.

**Sunday:** Final five stages including Hell's Gate Power Stage. Thierry attacks relentlessly. Ott defends with Nordic ice—every corner perfect, every landing precise. Gap fluctuates between 2.8s and 5.1s across four stages.

Power Stage showdown: Thierry needs stage win plus Ott finishing outside top-5 bonus positions. The Belgian ATTACKS. Jumps are massive, dust clouds biblical. He sets fastest time—but Ott finishes second on Power Stage, securing 4 bonus points. Ott wins Safari by 3.7 seconds.

**Final Classification:**

1. **Ott Tänak/Martin Järveoja (Hyundai)** - 3:52:18.3
2. **Thierry Neuville/Martijn Wydaeghe (Hyundai)** - +3.7s
3. **Kuro Nakamura/Akira Sato (Toyota)** - +2:45.8
4. **Sami Pajari/Enni Mälkönen (Toyota)** - +4:12.6
5. **Adrien Fourmaux/Alexandre Coria (M-Sport Ford)** - +6:38.2
DNF: **Grégoire Munster** (fire damage, SS12)

**Power Stage Winner:** Thierry Neuville (+5 bonus points)

**Post-Rally:** Ott sprays water (not champagne—limited supply in Kenya) from podium. His smile reaches "medium setting." Thierry fumes—"3.7 seconds! TROIS POINT SEPT!" Team restrains him from kicking tire barrier.

**Championship Standings After Round 3:**
1. Ott Tänak - 84 pts
2. Thierry Neuville - 51 pts
3. Kuro Nakamura - 48 pts
4. Sami Pajari - 37 pts
5. Adrien Fourmaux - 36 pts

---

### Round 4: Rally Croatia (April 17-20)

**Location:** Zagreb, Croatia
**Surface:** Smooth tarmac
**Stages:** 20 stages, 316.84 km competitive distance

**Pre-Event Drama:** First pure tarmac rally of season. Thierry arrives as tarmac specialist, determined to cut Ott's points lead. Kuro brings new hybrid deployment strategy specifically for tarmac. Adrien views Croatia as breakthrough opportunity—tarmac is his specialty.

**Rally Highlights:**

**Thursday:** Zagreb Arena super-special stage. Home-crowd favorite is Croatian wildcard entry Filip Mareš (local European Rally Cat), but stars dominate. Thierry sets fastest time, roars victory howl that echoes through arena. "This is MY surface!"

*Cat-themed incident:* Sami Pajari admits during press conference he's never actually driven on pure tarmac before joining WRC. Journalists stunned. "Finland is gravel or ice. Tarmac is...conceptual for me." Team panics. He finishes Thursday 8th overall.

**Friday:** Thierry unleashes tarmac mastery. His "Neuville hook" corner technique is devastating—late brake, rotate car on throttle, explosive exit. Takes six of eight stage wins. Ott trails by 14.2s after Day 1—uncharacteristic gap.

Kuro lurks in third, studying both leaders. His geometric precision works on tarmac, but lacks Thierry's raw attack speed. Adrien Fourmaux finally shows potential—sits fourth, just 8.3s behind Kuro.

*Cat-themed incident:* During SS6 (Vinski Vrh–Duga Resa), Grégoire Munster's rebuilt Puma suffers electrical gremlins. His radio fails mid-stage—no pacenotes from Louis. Rather than slow, Grégoire memorizes the road during first passage, drives second passage from memory. Somehow sets 5th-fastest time. Louis emerges from car pale and speechless.

**Saturday:** Croatian mountain roads provide stunning backdrop. Thierry continues domination but makes small error on SS13—clips inside barrier on tight left-hander, damages front-left suspension. Loses 12.4s plus mechanical integrity. Gap to Ott shrinks to 1.8s.

Adrien Fourmaux delivers career-defining performance. Takes two stage wins through surgical precision and aerodynamic understanding. Climbs to second overall, just 9.2s behind Thierry. "The Architect" is building a masterpiece.

*Cat-themed incident:* Kuro's black fur becomes tactical advantage on SS14. Stage runs through dense forest with dappled shade-sun patterns. His fur makes him nearly invisible in onboards, confusing following competitors trying to study his lines. Ott complains: "I cannot see where he brakes!"

**Sunday:** Three stages including Trakošćan Power Stage. Thierry leads Adrien by 9.2s, Ott by 11.5s. The Belgian needs to maintain composure despite damaged suspension.

SS18 proves decisive. Adrien attacks with French passion, takes 4.8s from wounded Thierry. Gap now just 4.4s with two stages remaining.

Final stage before Power Stage: Adrien commits EVERYTHING. His heterochromatic eyes locked in perfect focus, tail rigid with concentration. He sets fastest stage time, takes 5.1s from Thierry.

Adrien leads by 0.7 seconds entering Power Stage.

Power Stage drama: Both drivers attack desperately. Thierry's damaged suspension fights him every corner. Adrien drives the stage of his life—smooth, precise, maximizing hybrid power. He crosses finish line, waits...

Thierry finishes 1.2s slower.

**Adrien Fourmaux wins his first WRC rally by 1.9 seconds.**

The Turkish Van erupts from car, howling victory. M-Sport team swarms him. Malcolm Wilson sheds actual tear. Adrien's heterochromia eyes stream tears of different colors—amber eye produces golden tears, blue eye produces clear tears. It's poetic and bizarre.

**Final Classification:**

1. **Adrien Fourmaux/Alexandre Coria (M-Sport Ford)** - 2:51:42.5
2. **Thierry Neuville/Martijn Wydaeghe (Hyundai)** - +1.9s
3. **Ott Tänak/Martin Järveoja (Hyundai)** - +11.5s
4. **Kuro Nakamura/Akira Sato (Toyota)** - +38.7s
5. **Sami Pajari/Enni Mälkönen (Toyota)** - +2:22.8
6. **Grégoire Munster/Louis Louka (M-Sport Ford)** - +3:45.1

**Power Stage Winner:** Kuro Nakamura (+5 bonus points)

**Post-Rally:** Adrien quotes Voltaire from podium: "Perfection is attained by slow degrees; it requires the hand of time." Crowd cheers despite not understanding. Thierry congratulates him genuinely—"You drove like Belgian today. This is highest compliment."

**Championship Standings After Round 4:**
1. Ott Tänak - 99 pts
2. Thierry Neuville - 69 pts
3. Kuro Nakamura - 61 pts
4. Adrien Fourmaux - 61 pts (fewer wins, 4th on countback)
5. Sami Pajari - 49 pts

---

### Round 5: Vodafone Rally de Portugal (May 8-11)

**Location:** Coimbra, Portugal
**Surface:** Abrasive gravel, rocky mountain roads
**Stages:** 21 stages, 340.76 km competitive distance

**Pre-Event Drama:** Championship heats up. Ott's lead is comfortable but not unassailable. Adrien arrives with newfound confidence. Sami Pajari desperate to prove gravel speed after tarmac struggles. Toyota debuts new silver livery (replacing matte black) for Portugal onwards.

**Rally Highlights:**

**Thursday:** Coimbra super-special stage features cobblestone section that rattles cars violently. Grégoire Munster attacks so aggressively his helmet hits the roof. He finishes with slight concussion but sets third-fastest time. "Worth it," he grins, pupils dilated weirdly.

*Cat-themed incident:* Kuro appears in new silver GR Yaris for first time. His jet-black fur against silver livery creates stunning visual contrast. Photographers mob him during shakedown. "I liked invisible black," he mutters, uncomfortable with attention.

**Friday:** Portuguese gravel is wickedly abrasive—a tire-destroying lottery. Road position matters enormously; later starters face cleaner, faster lines.

Sami Pajari explodes from starting blocks with pure Finnish aggression. Takes three consecutive stage wins, catching everyone off-guard. His commitment through rocks is borderline reckless—"If I die, tell everyone I was sideways!" he radios after SS4.

Thierry struggles with road position (1st on road, sweeping loose gravel). Frustrated Korat yowls echo through Portuguese hills. Finishes Friday in 5th, fuming.

*Cat-themed incident:* During SS6 (Lousada), local Portuguese street cats line the roads, watching. Adrien's Turkish Van heritage activates—Vans are famous for social bonds with other cats. He waves at them between corners. Alexandre sighs: "Adrien, please concentrate on the rally, not making friends."

**Saturday:** Longest day—nine stages, 160km of brutal gravel. Sami maintains overnight lead but tire wear concerns grow. Kuro stalks in second, preserving tires with surgical precision.

SS11 (Amarante) proves pivotal. Sami attacks hard—too hard. Right-rear tire delaminates 3km from stage end. He limps to finish, loses 47 seconds. Kuro inherits lead by 8.3s.

Ott climbs steadily through field using Estonian efficiency. By Saturday evening sits third, just 15.2s off lead. Championship pressure mounting.

*Cat-themed incident:* Grégoire Munster challenges Sami to "who can get more sideways" competition during SS13. Both drivers go berserk—Scandinavian flicks so extreme cars briefly go fully sideways. Malcolm Wilson's radio message is unprintable. Both somehow set top-3 stage times. Malcolm's follow-up: "Gentlemen, I'm getting too old for this."

**Sunday:** Final four stages including Fafe Power Stage (legendary jump complex). Kuro leads Sami by 12.1s, Ott by 19.8s. The Shadow adopts conservative strategy—protect lead, accumulate points.

Sami attacks with nothing-to-lose aggression. Takes two stage wins but can't close gap to calculating Kuro. The Shadow's consistency proves superior to raw speed.

Power Stage at Fafe: Massive crowds watch iconic jump. Sami launches the GR Yaris into orbit—all four wheels 2+ meters airborne. Landing is violent but perfect. Sets Power Stage win by 1.7s. Crowd erupts.

Kuro crosses line, wins Rally Portugal by 8.9 seconds. Second victory of 2026 campaign. His celebration is subdued nod—"Expected result. Team performed well."

**Final Classification:**

1. **Kuro Nakamura/Akira Sato (Toyota)** - 3:18:45.7
2. **Sami Pajari/Enni Mälkönen (Toyota)** - +8.9s
3. **Ott Tänak/Martin Järveoja (Hyundai)** - +19.8s
4. **Thierry Neuville/Martijn Wydaeghe (Hyundai)** - +1:12.4
5. **Adrien Fourmaux/Alexandre Coria (M-Sport Ford)** - +1:45.6
6. **Grégoire Munster/Louis Louka (M-Sport Ford)** - +2:38.9

**Power Stage Winner:** Sami Pajari (+5 bonus points)

**Post-Rally:** Kuro's podium speech: "Consistency defeats luck. Thank you." Ten words. Crowd loves it. Sami bear-hugs him despite Kuro's visible discomfort. Toyota scores 1-2 finish—massive manufacturers' championship boost.

**Championship Standings After Round 5:**
1. Ott Tänak - 114 pts
2. Kuro Nakamura - 91 pts (30 pts gained!)
3. Thierry Neuville - 81 pts
4. Sami Pajari - 76 pts
5. Adrien Fourmaux - 71 pts

---

### Round 6: Rally Italia Sardegna (May 29-June 1)

**Location:** Alghero, Sardinia
**Surface:** Rocky, technical gravel
**Stages:** 21 stages, 331.68 km competitive distance

**Pre-Event Drama:** Mid-season approaches. Ott's championship lead narrowing—Kuro now just 23 points behind. Thierry desperate for victory to reignite campaign. Sardinia's technical stages and scorching heat create unique challenge.

**Rally Highlights:**

**Thursday:** Alghero waterfront super-special stage at sunset. Mediterranean backdrop stunning. Thierry sets fastest time, celebrating with dramatic victory yowl toward sea. Local Italian cats join chorus—creates surreal multi-species harmony.

*Cat-themed incident:* During reconnaissance, Grégoire discovers Sardinia's famous cork trees. Becomes obsessed. Sharpens claws on cork during service breaks. Malcolm Wilson confiscates cork samples: "You're a rally driver, not a furniture destroyer."

**Friday:** Sardinian rocks are merciless—sharp, tire-shredding granite everywhere. Adrien Fourmaux takes early lead through precise line choice and mechanical sympathy. His architectural approach: "Each rock is structural element requiring geometric consideration."

Kuro struggles with heat—his black Bombay fur absorbs sunlight despite silver car livery. Team installs additional cooling, provides ice packs during service. He drops to 5th by Friday afternoon.

*Cat-themed incident:* SS4 (Osilo–Tergu) features hairpin where Sami Pajari's aggressive entry breaks traction completely. Car rotates 180 degrees. Rather than reverse, he shouts "RALLY TECHNIQUE!" and continues backwards for 50 meters, then whips car forward again. Sets 4th-fastest time. Enni's voice through radio: "Sami. We discuss this later. Many discussions."

**Saturday:** Thierry unleashes championship-desperate aggression. Takes five stage wins through pure Belgian fury. His Korat reflexes are supernatural—catching slides milliseconds before disaster. Overtakes Adrien by 4.2s entering final Saturday stage.

Ott adopts conservative approach—protects car, preserves championship points. Sits safe in third, 28.4s off lead. Strategic, patient, Estonian.

*Cat-themed incident:* During SS14 (Coiluna–Loelle), Grégoire encounters shepherd with flock of sheep blocking road. Rather than wait, he weaves through flock at speed, sheep scattering dramatically. Radio message afterward: "Louis, add to pacenotes: 'Junction left, sheep maybe.'"

**Sunday:** Four stages including Sassari Power Stage. Thierry leads Adrien by 4.2s—razor-thin. Both drivers attacking desperately.

SS19 proves decisive: Thierry's aggression finally exceeds available grip. Carries too much speed into right-4, understeers into bank. Front-left suspension damaged. Limps to stage end, loses 38 seconds.

Adrien inherits lead by 33.8s. Final stages become victory procession. The French Architect drives calculated, conservative lines. Second victory of 2026—M-Sport's second of season.

**Final Classification:**

1. **Adrien Fourmaux/Alexandre Coria (M-Sport Ford)** - 3:12:22.8
2. **Ott Tänak/Martin Järveoja (Hyundai)** - +42.3s
3. **Thierry Neuville/Martijn Wydaeghe (Hyundai)** - +48.7s (after time loss)
4. **Sami Pajari/Enni Mälkönen (Toyota)** - +1:18.5
5. **Kuro Nakamura/Akira Sato (Toyota)** - +1:52.6
6. **Grégoire Munster/Louis Louka (M-Sport Ford)** - +2:45.3

**Power Stage Winner:** Thierry Neuville (+5 bonus points) - drives damaged car to Power Stage win through sheer anger

**Post-Rally:** Adrien dedicates victory to "all architects who understand that buildings and rally cars share fundamental principles of load distribution." Crowd baffled but enthusiastic. M-Sport celebration lasts until 3am—Malcolm Wilson seen dancing.

**Championship Standings After Round 6:**
1. Ott Tänak - 132 pts
2. Kuro Nakamura - 101 pts
3. Adrien Fourmaux - 96 pts (moves into podium position!)
4. Thierry Neuville - 101 pts (tied with Kuro, 3rd on countback)
5. Sami Pajari - 88 pts

---

### Round 7: Rally Poland (June 26-29)

**Location:** Mikołajki, Poland
**Surface:** Fast, smooth gravel
**Stages:** 19 stages, 302.74 km competitive distance

**Pre-Event Drama:** Championship tightening dramatically. Ott leads but advantage shrinking. Kuro and Thierry tied for second (31 points behind Ott). Adrien's consistency puts him in title hunt. Poland's ultra-fast stages favor brave commitment over precision.

**Rally Highlights:**

**Thursday:** Mikołajki lakeside super-special stage. Kuro sets fastest time through millimeter-precise line choice. His silver GR Yaris looks like liquid mercury under floodlights. Akira reports Kuro muttered "I miss black car" seventeen times during reconnaissance.

*Cat-themed incident:* Local Polish forest cats infiltrate service park overnight, sleep in warm rally car engines. Morning start delayed 15 minutes while mechanics gently evacuate cats. Grégoire adopts one, names it "Sideways." Malcolm Wilson: "No. Absolutely not." Grégoire smuggles it in luggage anyway.

**Friday:** Poland's speed is intoxicating—sixth-gear gravel sections at 190+ km/h. Sami Pajari in absolute element. Finnish aggression meets Polish smoothness. Takes four consecutive stage wins, driving with grin permanently affixed. "THIS IS WHAT RALLY SHOULD BE!" he roars.

Ott matches Sami's pace through Estonian precision. They trade stage wins all day. Gap between them fluctuates between 0.4s and 2.1s—championship battle intensifying.

*Cat-themed incident:* Thierry's Korat night-vision advantage becomes apparent on evening stage. SS7 runs through dense forest with twilight shadows. His luminous green eyes require less headlight reliance—drives partially on natural vision. Sets fastest time by 3.2s. "Finally my cat eyes are useful!"

**Saturday:** Epic Sami vs. Ott battle continues. Nine stages of pure commitment. Both drivers pushing physics' boundaries.

SS12 (Wieliczki) features infamous crest-before-left-5 combination. Sami attacks with trademark Finnish fearlessness—takes jump flat-out, lands sideways, catches slide perfectly, accelerates through corner. Sets stage record by 4.7s. His celebration howl echoes across Polish forests.

Ott responds with ice-cold perfection on SS13. No flash, no drama—just relentless speed through optimal efficiency. Takes back 2.8s.

*Cat-themed incident:* Adrien's architectural tendencies activate during SS14. Spots interesting wooden barn mid-stage, begins analyzing its structural integrity while approaching corner. Alexandre: "ADRIEN! CORNER!" Adrien snaps back, makes corner perfectly: "Sorry, but did you see those support beams? Fascinating load distribution."

**Sunday:** Three stages including Mikolajki Power Stage. Sami leads Ott by 3.8s. Championship implications enormous—if Sami wins, he cuts Ott's lead to massive 26 points.

SS18: Ott attacks with everything. Corners are taken at limit. His Estonian composure fractures slightly—visible aggression in driving style. He takes 2.2s from Sami. Gap now 1.6s entering Power Stage.

Power Stage: Both drivers push beyond sanity. Sami commits to jumps with abandon. Ott matches him through superior tire preservation. Final kilometer: Sami ahead by 0.8s on splits...

Final corner (fast right-5 into finish): Sami cuts too tight, clips inside bank with left-front, momentarily loses momentum. Ott's line is millimeter-perfect. They cross finish line...

**Ott Tänak wins by 0.3 seconds.**

Photo finish. Sami slumps in car, devastated. Ott's celebration is slight fist-pump—biggest emotion ever recorded.

**Final Classification:**

1. **Ott Tänak/Martin Järveoja (Hyundai)** - 2:38:52.1
2. **Sami Pajari/Enni Mälkönen (Toyota)** - +0.3s (heartbreak)
3. **Thierry Neuville/Martijn Wydaeghe (Hyundai)** - +12.8s
4. **Kuro Nakamura/Akira Sato (Toyota)** - +28.4s
5. **Adrien Fourmaux/Alexandre Coria (M-Sport Ford)** - +45.7s
6. **Grégoire Munster/Louis Louka (M-Sport Ford)** - +1:32.6

**Power Stage Winner:** Sami Pajari (+5 bonus points) - small consolation

**Post-Rally:** Sami cries openly on podium. "0.3 seconds. Point three." Ott approaches, places paw on Sami's shoulder: "You drove like champion today. Was good fight." They embrace. Moment goes viral—#CatWRCRespect trends globally.

**Championship Standings After Round 7:**
1. Ott Tänak - 162 pts
2. Kuro Nakamura - 113 pts
3. Thierry Neuville - 116 pts (2nd on countback)
4. Adrien Fourmaux - 108 pts
5. Sami Pajari - 111 pts (5th on countback)

---

## Mid-Season Analysis & Team Situations

**Championship Battle:** Ott's lead is commanding but not unassailable. His consistency is devastating—podium in every round. But Kuro, Thierry, Sami, and Adrien all remain within mathematical striking distance. Second half of season features nine rounds—216 points available. Title fight alive.

**Toyota Situation:** The reigning champions find themselves in peculiar position. Kuro's consistency keeps him in hunt, but Sami's raw speed lacks finishing reliability. Team dynamics strain slightly—Sami's youth vs. Kuro's experience creates subtle tension. Silver livery hasn't resolved Kuro's heat absorption issues on hot rallies.

**Hyundai Situation:** Two-pronged attack strategy works brilliantly. Ott leads championship through Estonian efficiency. Thierry provides spectacular stage wins and emotional energy. But Thierry's championship drought continues—he's fastest but not most consistent. Internal team dynamics remain professional but competitive.

**M-Sport Situation:** Privateer heroes punching above weight. Adrien's two victories and championship position are miracle for limited budget. Grégoire provides entertainment value and occasional brilliance but needs consistency. Malcolm Wilson's tactical genius maximizes every resource.

---

*[Season continues with Rounds 8-13. Additional rally details, cat-themed incidents, and championship conclusion available for expansion. Lorebook established with detailed character foundations, team dynamics, and narrative threads for further development.]*

