# Cat World Rally Championship (CWRC) - Complete Glossary

## Rally-Specific Terms

All-Wheel-Drive (AWD): Drivetrain system powering all four wheels simultaneously, essential for Rally1 cars, provides traction on loose surfaces
Chicane: Series of tight alternating corners designed to slow cars, common in super-special stages
Co-Driver: Navigator who reads pacenotes to driver, critical partnership requiring absolute trust
Competitive Distance: Total length of timed special stages, excludes transport sections between stages
Crest: Rise in road creating jump or loss of traction at apex, requires commitment and car control
DNF (Did Not Finish): Retirement from rally due to accident, mechanical failure, or inability to continue
FIA (Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile): Global governing body for motorsport, sets CWRC regulations
Fesh-fesh: Fine volcanic dust found in African rallies, reduces visibility and traction dramatically
Hairpin: 180-degree corner requiring significant speed reduction, numbered as "1" in pacenote system
Homologation: Official certification that rally car meets technical regulations, required for competition
Hybrid Deployment: Strategic use of electric motor boost via driver-controlled button, limited by battery capacity
Jump: Stage section where car becomes airborne, requires proper landing technique and setup
Liason Section: Non-competitive transport between special stages, timed but not racing
Line (Racing Line): Optimal path through corner maximizing speed while minimizing distance, geometric precision required
Pacenotes: Co-driver's coded instructions describing upcoming corners, distances, and hazards
Power Stage: Final stage offering bonus championship points (5-4-3-2-1 for top-5 finishers)
Rally1: Top category in CWRC featuring hybrid cars producing 500hp combined output
Recce (Reconnaissance): Pre-rally stage inspection where crews prepare pacenotes, limited passes allowed
Road Position: Starting order determined by championship standings, critical on gravel (later = cleaner)
Scandinavian Flick: Weight transfer technique where car is steered away from corner before flicking toward apex
Scrutineering: Technical inspection ensuring cars meet regulations, conducted pre-rally and post-rally
Service Park: Designated area where mechanics work on cars between stages, strict time limits
Shakedown: Non-competitive test stage before rally begins, final setup verification
Special Stage (SS): Competitive timed section on closed roads, rally consists of multiple SS
Stage Time: Official time recorded for special stage, determines overall classification
Super-Special Stage: Short competitive stage in stadium or city streets, spectator-friendly
Sump Guard: Protective plate under car preventing rock damage to engine/gearbox, mandatory equipment
Transport Section: Road travel between special stages, follows traffic laws, not competitive
WRC Promoter: Commercial rights holder organizing calendar, broadcasting, sponsorship

## Technical Terms

1.6L Turbo Engine: Rally1 engine specification, produces approximately 380 horsepower before hybrid assist
Active Differential: Electronically-controlled center differential distributing power between axles
Anti-Lag System: Keeps turbocharger spooled between throttle applications, maintains boost pressure
Dampers: Adjustable shock absorbers controlling suspension movement, 48-way adjustable in Rally1
Differential: Mechanical device allowing wheels to rotate at different speeds during cornering
Hybrid System: Combination of combustion engine and electric motor, Rally1 standard is 100kW electric unit
Limited-Slip Differential (LSD): Mechanical differential limiting wheel speed difference, improves traction
Paddle-Shift: Steering wheel-mounted controls for sequential gearbox, faster than manual gear lever
Regenerative Braking: Energy recovery system charging hybrid battery during braking, driver-controlled deployment
Roll Cage: Steel safety structure protecting occupants during crashes, FIA-homologated construction
Sequential Gearbox: Racing transmission requiring gears to be selected in order, faster shifting than manual
Spaceframe Chassis: Tubular steel construction providing structural rigidity, basis for Rally1 cars
Spool (Turbocharger): Turbo rotation speed building boost pressure, anti-lag maintains spool
Suspension Travel: Distance suspension can compress/extend, Rally1 features long travel for rough terrain
Telemetry: Real-time data transmission from car to team, 150+ sensor channels monitored
Weight Distribution: Balance of car's mass front-to-rear, Rally1 targets approximately 50/50

## Feline-Specific Terms

Bombay Cat: Black-furred breed with copper eyes, superior night vision, heat-sensitive (Kuro, Sami)
Digitigrade: Walking on toes rather than flat-footed, feline leg structure requiring custom pedal boxes
Heterochromia: Condition where eyes are different colors, common in Turkish Van breed (Adrien's amber/blue eyes)
Kaizen: Japanese philosophy of continuous improvement, Toyota team culture, embodied by Kuro
Korat Cat: Silver-blue breed with green eyes from Thailand, cold-sensitive but excellent reflexes (Thierry, Ott)
Omamori: Japanese amulet for good fortune, Kuro keeps one from Ise Grand Shrine in helmet
Tapetum Lucidum: Reflective layer behind retina providing superior night vision, all cats possess
Turkish Van Cat: White breed with colored markings, water-resistant coat, heterochromia common (Adrien, Grégoire)
Whiskers: Sensory organs providing environmental feedback, Rally1 cockpits designed with whisker clearance zones

## Team-Specific Terms

Corner Rascals: Nickname for Hyundai team, reflects Thierry's aggressive "rascal" driving style
GR (Gazoo Racing): Toyota's motorsport division, name appears on Yaris Rally1 cars
Kaizen: Continuous improvement philosophy central to Toyota team culture
M-Sport: Privateer team running Ford rally program, based in Cumbria, England, limited budget requires creativity
Manufacturers' Championship: Team competition where top 3 cars from each manufacturer score points
Privateer: Team operating without full factory manufacturer support, M-Sport is competitive privateer
Service Crew: Mechanics working on cars during service parks, typically 4-5 per car
Team Principal: Highest authority within team, makes strategic decisions during rallies
The Architect: Adrien Fourmaux's nickname, reflects his analytical approach treating rally as engineering problem
The Ice King: Ott Tänak's nickname, reflects his emotionally minimal, brutally efficient driving style
The Shadow: Kuro Nakamura's nickname, reflects jet-black fur and stealthy consistent speed
The Warrior: Thierry Neuville's nickname, reflects passionate aggressive attacking driving style
The Wildcat: Grégoire Munster's nickname, reflects fearless chaotic joyful approach to rallying
Wildcard: Sami Pajari's nickname, reflects unpredictable mix of brilliant speed and occasional mistakes

## Rally Location Terms

Acropolis: Greek rally known as roughest in championship, extreme heat and rock-strewn stages
Alghero: Sardinian rally base, features sharp granite rocks and Mediterranean heat
Col de Turini: Legendary Monte-Carlo mountain pass, mixed ice/snow/tarmac conditions
Fafe: Portuguese stage complex featuring massive jumps, iconic rally imagery location
Gap: French Alpine town serving as Monte-Carlo rally headquarters
Jyväskylä: Finnish rally base, fastest rally in championship with massive jumps
Monte-Carlo: Season-opening rally in French Alps/Monaco, most unpredictable conditions
Nairobi: Kenyan rally base, Safari Rally features wildlife encounters and brutal durability test
Sardinia: Italian island rally featuring technical gravel and tire-destroying rocks
Service Park: Temporary rally headquarters erected for each event, includes garages and hospitality
Super-Special Stage: Spectator-friendly short stage in stadiums or city centers
Sweden: Winter rally in Umeå featuring frozen lakes and extreme cold (-20°C)
Tarmac Rally: Events on sealed asphalt roads (Croatia, Japan, Central Europe)
Umeå: Swedish Arctic city hosting winter rally, frozen lake stages

## Championship & Scoring Terms

Championship Leader: Driver with most points, currently Ott Tänak with 162 points after Round 7
Countback: Tiebreaker using number of wins when drivers have equal points
DNF (Did Not Finish): Retirement from rally, scores zero points, costly for championship
Gap: Points difference between drivers, Ott leads second place by 46 points
Manufacturers' Points: Top 3 cars from each manufacturer score toward team championship
Mathematical Possibility: Driver can still win championship if maximum points scored and rivals falter
Podium: Top 3 finishing positions, critical for championship points (25-18-15)
Points Finish: Any position 1st through 10th scores championship points
Power Stage Points: Bonus points (5-4-3-2-1) awarded for fastest times on final stage
Three-Peat: Winning championship three consecutive years, Kuro attempting 2024-2025-2026

## Cultural & Social Terms

Belgian Fury: Thierry Neuville's passionate aggressive driving style representing Belgian motorsport culture
Cat World Rally Championship (CWRC): Alternate Earth championship where anthropomorphic cats are dominant species
Death Metal: Finnish metal music genre, Sami Pajari's pre-stage soundtrack of choice
FIA 8860-2018: Helmet safety standard, mandatory for CWRC competition
Finnish Rally Religion: Cultural phenomenon where rally is treated with religious reverence in Finland
HANS Device: Head And Neck Support safety device, mandatory in CWRC
Night Vision Advantage: Feline tapetum lucidum provides 6-8x better low-light vision than humans
Rally Family: Cultural bond between competitors who race hard but celebrate together
Respect Culture: CWRC tradition where rivals compete fiercely but maintain mutual respect
Shinto Blessing: Japanese religious ceremony, Kuro performs at shrines before rallies
Victory Howl: Feline vocalization celebrating stage wins, culturally accepted in CWRC
Yowl: Loud feline vocalization, Thierry's Korat howl echoes through service parks after victories

## Mechanical & Setup Terms

Brake Balance: Adjustable distribution of braking force front-to-rear, driver-controlled via cockpit dial
Damper Setting: Suspension adjustment controlling compression/rebound rates, 48-way adjustable
Differential Setting: Electronic adjustment of power distribution between axles, affects handling balance
Ride Height: Ground clearance adjustment, increased for rough rallies, decreased for tarmac
Spring Rate: Suspension spring stiffness, multiple options selected per rally surface
Tire Allocation: Regulated number of tires per rally (28 for gravel, 24 for tarmac)
Tire Compound: Pirelli offers soft/hard gravel, soft/hard/wet tarmac, studded/non-studded snow
Tire Degradation: Wear rate affecting performance, critical management skill
Tire Pressure: Regulated minimum 1.6 bar, affects grip and durability
Wing Angle: Adjustable rear aerodynamic element, limited driver-controlled range

## Co-Driver & Navigation Terms

Calculator: Enni Mälkönen's nickname, reflects mathematical approach to pace notes
Crest-Call: Pacenote warning of upcoming rise/jump in road
Distance Call: Meters to next corner called by co-driver, critical for braking point
Echo: Akira Sato's nickname, reflects rhythmic pacenote delivery style
Gravel Crew: Stage workers who sweep loose gravel from road after early starters
Intercom: Communication system between driver and co-driver, critical during stages
Junction: Intersection during stage, often tightens corner radius unexpectedly
Left-5/Right-4: Pacenote corner notation, numbers 1-6 indicate severity (1=hairpin, 6=flat-out)
Long/Short: Pacenote modifiers describing corner length or duration
Oracle: Alexandre Coria's nickname, reflects ability to predict setup needs before driver realizes
Pace Note Book: Co-driver's annotated stage guide, color-coded and meticulously organized
Professor: Louis Louka's nickname, reflects patient educational approach to co-driving
Tightens: Pacenote warning that corner radius decreases mid-turn, requires additional braking
Tulip Diagram: Visual representation of junctions in pace notes, resembles tulip flower shape
Viking: Martin Järveoja's nickname, reflects Estonian heritage and distinctive beard styling

## Media & Broadcasting Terms

Onboard Camera: In-car camera showing driver's perspective, popular for broadcast
Rally TV: Official CWRC broadcasting service, 24/7 coverage during rally weekends
Stage-End Interview: Media interview immediately after stage finish, captures emotional reactions
Timing Screen: Real-time display of stage times and overall classification
WRC+: Subscription streaming service offering live stage coverage and onboards

## Miscellaneous Terms

0.3 Seconds: Margin by which Ott beat Sami in Poland Round 7, heartbreaking for Sami
847 Entries: Number of incidents in Louis Louka's journal documenting Grégoire's wild moments
Quackers: Grégoire Munster's lucky rubber duck kept on dashboard
Sideways: Driving technique where car drifts through corners, Sami and Grégoire's competition
Silver Livery: Toyota's 2026 car color replacing previous matte black, Kuro dislikes the change
Three-Point-Seven: Margin (seconds) of Ott's Safari Kenya victory over Thierry, source of Belgian frustration

---END OF GLOSSARY---
```]]></Content>
